<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Coriosolites</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 56 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Armorican people of northern Brittany, best known today for huge hoards of their distinctive billon coins found in Jersey.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Coriosolites lived in the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotes-darmor">Côtes-d'Armor</a>, around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rance">Rance</a> estuary. Caesar names them among the Armorican peoples who submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-licinius-crassus">Publius Crassus</a> in 57 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They issued abundant coins of base <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> with stylised heads and horses. Hoards buried around the time of the Roman conquest, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grouville-hoard">Grouville hoard</a> found in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Jersey</a> in 2012 with around 70,000 coins, suggest wealth from cross-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">Channel</a> trade or refugees fleeing Caesar.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veneti">Veneti</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/unelli">Unelli</a> in 56 BCE they were absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-lugdunensis">Gallia Lugdunensis</a>. Their Roman capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corseul">Fanum Martis</a> at Corseul, with a large temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mars-mythology">Mars</a>, and the name survives in Corseul.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/coriosolites.jpg" alt="Coriosolites" width="320" height="647"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curiosolites_coinage_5th_1st_century_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 56 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Curiosolitae</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-178&amp;focus=coriosolites" aria-label="Coriosolites on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4728 553 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Coriosolites lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5048 809h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5048 809h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day France. <a href="/atlas?year=-178&amp;focus=coriosolites">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

